WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
How do you test what drives a living cell from inside?

2
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.960
Watching from outside shows events happening together, not cause.

3
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.800
To test cause directly, place a molecule, nucleus, or cytoplasm straight inside one

4
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:15.800
cell.

5
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.680
Start with microinjection.

6
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.500
A fine glass needle pierces one cell, placing a molecule into its cytoplasm, outside

7
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:32.560
the nucleus. That skips the steps outside the cell and tests what that molecule does

8
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.200
inside. A marker dye rides along to identify every injected cell.

9
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.980
Other questions need more than one molecule.

10
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.520
In nuclear transfer, you remove or destroy an egg's own nucleus and place another

11
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.960
cell's nucleus inside.

12
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.720
In cell fusion, you merge two cells so two nuclei share one cytoplasm.

13
00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.660
All three share one rule: put a part inside a living cell to test what it drives.

14
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.570
Now test how to read a result.

15
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:10.690
Suppose you put a tadpole gut nucleus into an egg whose own nucleus was destroyed.

16
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:16.890
Most eggs fail to develop, and only a small fraction become normal tadpoles.

17
00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:23.190
Do those failures show specialized nuclei had discarded developmental genes?

18
00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:33.373
No. Failures alone cannot show what was missing.

19
00:01:34.673 --> 00:01:40.453
Each normal tadpole shows that specialized nucleus kept the needed instructions, and

20
00:01:40.453 --> 00:01:42.213
egg cytoplasm could read them.

21
00:01:43.413 --> 00:01:47.073
A genetic nuclear marker rules out a surviving egg nucleus.

22
00:01:48.473 --> 00:01:53.213
Still, low success left doubt that successes came from rare unspecialized cells.

23
00:01:54.317 --> 00:01:59.617
Cell fusion needs its own controls, because merging cells might itself trigger or

24
00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:05.917
block a response. Pre-labelling one partner with a nuclear dye identifies every

25
00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:07.837
nucleus inside the fused cell.

26
00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:14.217
Same-phase fusions and unfused neighbours on the same slide test whether fusion

27
00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:15.557
itself changes anything.

28
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.250
With controls in place, these methods unlocked three landmark discoveries.

29
00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:29.510
In 1962 at Oxford, John Gurdon put tadpole intestinal nuclei into Xenopus laevis

30
00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.650
frog eggs lacking nuclei.

31
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:37.150
A small fraction became normal feeding tadpoles, nervous system included.

32
00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.990
The gut-cell nucleus still held instructions for a whole animal.

33
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:49.220
Second, in 1970 Rao and Johnson fused cultured HeLa cells from different phases,

34
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.160
sharing one cytoplasm.

35
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.700
Beside an S-phase cell copying DNA, an uncopied G1 nucleus started replicating

36
00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.400
early. A copied G2 nucleus did not.

37
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.100
As later reviews describe, S-phase cytoplasm can start replication.

38
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:13.240
Under these fusion conditions a replicated nucleus did not.

39
00:03:14.333 --> 00:03:20.713
Third, in 1992 Anne Ridley and Alan Hall injected purified proteins into

40
00:03:20.713 --> 00:03:22.473
serum-starved fibroblasts.

41
00:03:23.713 --> 00:03:25.673
Those cells start with few stress fibres.

42
00:03:27.153 --> 00:03:31.613
Active Rho filled them with thick actin cables, while active Rac made their edges

43
00:03:31.613 --> 00:03:38.273
ruffle. Putting molecules, nuclei, or cytoplasm straight into living cells shows

44
00:03:38.273 --> 00:03:39.913
what controls each response.
